Fillets: a rounding whose contact on a face lies along a part of another
rounding's contact there, rides on that part (mitcad#1392). Example: a boss
of radius 4.5 standing 2 out of a bar's end face, coaxial with the bar's
half-round top (one cylinder face), its foot (the half circle below the
top) and its rim (the whole circle) rounded together with 1, half the
boss's height: both contacts on the cylinder lie on the circle at its
middle, the foot's open contact along part of the rim's closed one.
0153 and 0270 record such a pair only where the two contacts are one line
both ways and neither stripe is closed, so ChFi3d_StripeEdgeInter found
the common segment, which covers only part of the rim's contact, and threw
("fillets have too big radiuses").

- ChFi3d_Builder::Compute, where 0153 and 0270 record their pairs: an open
  stripe whose contact on the face (its ends inside it or on its seam),
  or each of its SurfData's contacts there, lies along a part of the
  other stripe's contact (partOfContact; the other's may be closed) is
  recorded as a ride on the other.
- ApplyRides: each SurfData of the rider on the face, its ends as the
  corners left them, rides on its own piece of the other's line: the
  other's SurfData holding its contact is split at its ends (splitForPart,
  with 0271's splitSurfDataAt), and the rider leaves the face there as in
  0270. The face keeps its part along the rest of the other's line.
- The piece's ends inside the other's line, and the rider's ends at the
  junctions of its SurfData, are no ends of the stripes: each stripe makes
  its own point there, made one once both are in the data structure, and
  the shared line keeps one start and one end, the piece's
  (keepPartLineEnds). Where the rider runs against the line, that is told
  from its ends.
- On the face's seam, where the rider ends at the start of the other's
  closed line or crosses the seam, each stripe cut the seam as if alone,
  keeping the face on the side the other consumes: the seam keeps the face
  on a side only where both keep it (combineSeamPoints, joinSeamPoints).
